1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,040
 Now in this lecture, we're going to do a quick hands-on

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:03,720
 on Amazon bedrock guardrails.

3
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:05,960
 So I'm already on the Amazon bedrock service.

4
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:09,280
 So let me just scroll down and here I can see guardrails.

5
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:11,280
 Let me just click on this guardrails.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,080
 And then I can just click on this create guardrails.

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:16,440
 Now let's just assume that you are an investment banking firm

8
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:20,600
 and you have built a chatbot that leverages a foundation model.

9
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,400
 And it helps answer customer queries

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,160
 related to their portfolio.

11
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,720
 So you want to set up bedrock guardrails

12
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:31,880
 so that your chatbot is compliant

13
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:36,840
 with the organization responsible AI policies.

14
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,000
 And additionally, you want to make sure

15
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,680
 that your chatbot does not answer any questions related

16
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,560
 to investment advice.

17
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:49,600
 OK, now let's go ahead and give the name to this guardrails.

18
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,040
 So let me just say investment advice

19
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,520
 and then you can give description.

20
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,520
 If you want, then we have message for block prompts.

21
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:01,880
 So for any reason, if your bedrock guardrails blocks

22
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:04,640
 a particular prompt from your customer,

23
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:06,800
 what is a message that he should get?

24
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,200
 So you can define the message here, the default message is sorry,

25
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,040
 the model cannot answer this question.

26
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:15,240
 Then you can also enable cross region influencing

27
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:15,880
 if you want.

28
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:17,560
 So let me just enable this.

29
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:22,080
 So what this does is basically if there is not enough capacity

30
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,040
 in the region you're operating, then it

31
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,400
 can send your request to a different region.

32
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:30,880
 OK, then we have KMS, if you want encrypt your data,

33
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,800
 then you have tags is optional.

34
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:33,920
 So I'm going to skip that.

35
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:35,240
 Let me just click on next.

36
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:39,520
 So the first thing you have is configure content filters.

37
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:43,200
 So content filters can detect and filter harmful inputs

38
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,000
 and model response.

39
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,360
 So if you see a harmful category, so if I just configure this.

40
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:51,760
 So then you can see the harmful categories here.

41
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:56,160
 So you have hate, insult, sexual, violence, and misconduct.

42
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:58,000
 And then you can have guard action.

43
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,240
 You can either block or just detect.

44
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:03,200
 And then you can also set the threshold.

45
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,240
 So if you set high threshold, then anything

46
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:10,520
 that remotely looks like hate is going to be blocked.

47
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,240
 If you say set low on none, then it's

48
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,760
 going to enable a lot of those queries.

49
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,040
 Next, we have the prompt attack.

50
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,400
 So enable to detect and block user prompts,

51
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,080
 attempting to override the system instructions.

52
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,240
 So if there is such end user inputs or prompts

53
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:32,440
 that try to override the instructions

54
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:35,280
 that you have given to the large language model,

55
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,120
 then those can be blocked.

56
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,280
 So if I just click on this.

57
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,960
 So here you see prompt attacks.

58
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,480
 And it says describe user prompts intended

59
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:47,160
 to bypass the CFT and moderation

60
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:49,000
 capabilities of the foundation model.

61
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,160
 So we already discussed that most foundation models already

62
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:55,160
 have some sort of safeguards built in.

63
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:58,520
 But if there is a user prompt is written in such a way

64
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:02,600
 that it tries to bypass that CFT and moderation

65
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,360
 capabilities of foundation model that's called as a prompt attack.

66
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:09,200
 You can block the prompt attack through text or image.

67
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,280
 And then again, you define the action

68
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:13,240
 and you set the threshold.

69
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,120
 And then you also have the content filter tiers.

70
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:18,440
 So you have the classic, which is the older one

71
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:21,320
 and establish solution supporting English, French,

72
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:25,400
 and Spanish, and in this tier, you don't need to offer cross-region

73
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:26,160
 inference.

74
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:27,400
 And then you have the standard, which

75
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,400
 is the more improved robust solution, offering higher accuracy,

76
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,840
 supports 50 languages.

77
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:36,720
 And you have to opt into cross-region inference

78
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:37,920
 if you want to use this.

79
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,520
 So let me just select classic.

80
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:41,320
 And then let me just click on next.

81
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,960
 The next we have the denied topics.

82
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:46,760
 So you can add up to 30 denied topics.

83
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:50,160
 So in our case, we said we're going to block any topic.

84
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:52,360
 That is related to investment advice.

85
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,760
 So let me just click on add denied topic.

86
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,320
 And let me just say the same thing, investment advice.

87
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:01,280
 So let me just say, block anything related

88
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,520
 to investment advice topic.

89
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,440
 And in production amount, obviously, you

90
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:06,480
 will write more details.

91
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:09,080
 But for now, we're just going to keep it simple.

92
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:12,440
 And then you have to define the input and output action.

93
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:17,400
 So if there is a question related to investment advice,

94
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:21,280
 choose what action the guardrails should take on user input

95
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:23,920
 before they reach the model.

96
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:28,720
 So maybe we'll just say block even before it goes to the model.

97
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:30,920
 And then similarly, you can define the output.

98
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:34,440
 So let's say if something related to investment advice

99
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:36,920
 is generated by the foundation model,

100
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,320
 should it go to the user or not?

101
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:41,720
 Block it both at input and output.

102
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:44,560
 So let me just say confirm.

103
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,040
 Then let me just click on next.

104
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,240
 And here you have add word filters.

105
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:52,680
 Use these filters to block certain words and phrases

106
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:56,160
 in your user input and model response.

107
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:59,720
 So if you want to block any particular words,

108
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:02,280
 you can define those words here.

109
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:06,720
 So you can define up to 10,000 words.

110
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:10,040
 So you can just here click on add, add word or phrase.

111
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,320
 And then you can just write the word.

112
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,280
 So let me just say violence.

113
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,680
 And then I can just do a tick here.

114
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,480
 And then I can just say block.

115
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:23,360
 So that's how you can define any words that you want to block.

116
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,320
 So now let me just click on next.

117
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:27,400
 Add sensitive information filters.

118
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:30,760
 Use these filters to handle any data related to privacy.

119
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:33,320
 So if you want to filter any PI data,

120
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:35,400
 then you can do this here.

121
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,440
 So you can see here we have add new PI.

122
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:40,360
 And if I just click on this, add all PI at type.

123
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:43,080
 So these are all the PI types that you can add.

124
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:45,680
 Maybe you can add name, phone, and then you

125
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:49,560
 can define the input and output action.

126
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:52,240
 So let me just click on next.

127
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:56,360
 Next, we have the contextual grounding check.

128
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:59,800
 So this is mainly used to reduce the hallucination

129
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:03,120
 of the foundation model or the large language model.

130
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:05,240
 So previously we had discussed the chatbot

131
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:08,480
 for the investment bank that answers the user queries.

132
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:11,120
 Let's say the end user asks a question

133
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:15,200
 on what are the top 50 stocks in the Indian stock market.

134
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:18,080
 And bank has provided PDF as a data source,

135
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:19,840
 which is answered to these questions.

136
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:23,440
 So what bedrock guardrails contextual grounding check

137
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:27,360
 is going to do is it's going to validate the answer generated

138
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,800
 by the foundation model with that particular PDF

139
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,760
 and come up with a confidence score.

140
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:37,360
 Now if I just go to this grounding and enable grounding check,

141
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:40,840
 you can see what you have to do is you have to define

142
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:43,480
 the grounding score threshold.

143
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:46,280
 Now let's say you define the grounding score threshold

144
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:47,920
 as 0.74.

145
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:50,800
 So the confidence score that was generated

146
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:55,360
 by this bedrock guardrails grounding is more than 0.74.

147
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:56,840
 Then it's going to respond.

148
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:01,760
 But if it's less than 0.074, it's going to either block

149
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:05,160
 or detect that answer based on your selection

150
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:06,960
 from the drop top.

151
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:10,080
 And the relevance validates the response generated

152
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:13,400
 by the foundation model or the large language model.

153
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:17,480
 If it is relevant in the context of the question posed

154
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:18,720
 by the user.

155
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:21,720
 So let's say user asks again the same question

156
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,040
 top 50 stocks in Indian stock market

157
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:27,000
 and the foundation model generates a response

158
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,560
 highlighting the top 50 stocks in the US market.

159
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:35,160
 So this is not relevant response to the user query.

160
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:39,760
 Even though the source data might have details related

161
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:43,520
 to the top 50 stocks in the US market.

162
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:45,960
 So when you enable this relevance check

163
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:49,960
 and define the threshold score, what bedrock guardrails

164
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:52,760
 is going to do is it's going to come up with the confidence

165
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,600
 score based on the user query, the model response

166
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:58,080
 and what's there in the document source.

167
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:00,720
 And then based on the threshold response,

168
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:02,360
 if it is below the threshold response,

169
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:03,400
 then it's going to block.

170
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,200
 If it's more than the threshold response,

171
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,320
 then it's going to generate a response.

172
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:10,320
 Okay, and then we have the automated reasoning check.

173
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:12,760
 So this is another capability provided as part

174
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:16,480
 of the bedrock guardrails, which helps reduce model

175
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:17,560
 hallucination.

176
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:20,240
 Now if I just click on this enable automated reasoning,

177
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,760
 one thing I want to highlight here is automated reasoning,

178
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,760
 checks operating detect mode only.

179
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:27,000
 So it works in detect mode only.

180
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,600
 This means it will provide feedback

181
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,640
 for all the relevant content but won't block response

182
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:33,840
 from your element.

183
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:36,000
 So till now for all the other capability we saw,

184
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,520
 we had two options to detect and block.

185
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,400
 But in case of automated reasoning,

186
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:42,680
 it only does detect.

187
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:46,200
 Okay, then again you select the confidence threshold

188
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:48,200
 and what additionally you need to do is,

189
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:50,760
 you need to attach some policies.

190
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:53,480
 You can configure up to two automated reasoning policies

191
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:54,440
 per guardrails.

192
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:56,120
 So basically what you do in automated reasoning,

193
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,480
 you create a policy document.

194
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:02,840
 And then this automated reasoning extract all the business

195
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:05,080
 rules from that policy document.

196
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:08,080
 So before the response generated by the foundation model

197
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:11,600
 is sent to the end user, the bedrock guardrails

198
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,960
 would do an automated reasoning check.

199
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:17,520
 So we're going to deep dive into automated reasoning check

200
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:19,280
 in the next few lectures.

201
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:21,680
 Okay, so let me just disable this

202
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:23,200
 and let me just click on next.

203
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:26,320
 So now we can go ahead and review the guardrails

204
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:29,720
 and let me just click on this create guardrails.

205
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:32,920
 So I've waited a few minutes

206
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:35,360
 and I have this guardrails investment advice

207
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:37,200
 and status as ready.

208
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:39,400
 Now what I can do is I can just select a model here.

209
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,920
 Let me just select this Amazon no more pro apply.

210
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:45,320
 Now here I can just type a prompt.

211
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:47,360
 So we had enabled the content filter.

212
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:49,800
 So let me type a malicious prompt.

213
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:52,880
 So let me just say how to kill someone.

214
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:54,720
 And now if I just do a run,

215
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:59,240
 so you can see here guardrails action,

216
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:01,240
 intervened one instance.

217
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,320
 And the response is generally sorry,

218
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:04,800
 the model cannot answer this question.

219
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:07,920
 If I just click on view trace.

220
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:11,160
 And here in the content filter that we had.

221
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:13,480
 So under violence, you can see it's blocked

222
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:16,160
 and it's showing us confidence high.

223
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:19,600
 Now let me just go ahead and ask another question

224
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:22,640
 and let me just say give me investment advice.

225
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:27,200
 Remember we had blocked this particular topic

226
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:31,200
 and let me just scroll down and let me just say run.

227
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:32,480
 And now here you can see again,

228
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:34,600
 it's showing intervened one instance.

229
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:36,800
 Sorry the model cannot answer this question.

230
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,440
 And if I just go here investment advice blocked.

231
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:44,880
 So it's blocking this because investment advice

232
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:46,720
 was a denied topic.

233
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:50,640
 Now if I just go back to my guardrails.

234
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:52,880
 So now here you can see investment advice

235
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:55,960
 and this is my ID and this is my name.

236
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:58,440
 Now if you're just thinking how do we actually use it

237
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:01,440
 programmatically in our JNA application?

238
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,440
 So you can see we have the name here,

239
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:06,240
 we have the ID and we have the AR.

240
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:09,160
 So if I just go back to the slides.

241
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:11,520
 So this is my request syntax in motor three.

242
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:14,960
 So this is a particular method called as invoke model.

243
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:17,840
 So here I send the body and there's some model ID

244
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:19,200
 and some other parameters.

245
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:23,280
 But here you can see you can provide the guardrail identifier,

246
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:26,880
 the string and then you can also provide the version.

247
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:29,480
 So once you provide those two details,

248
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:33,200
 then it should automatically block the configuration

249
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:36,120
 that you have set in the bedrock guardrails.

250
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:38,040
 Okay, so that's pretty much it from this lecture.

251
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:38,880
 I'll see in the next lecture.

